<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saadat Ali Khan I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1680–<a href="/years/1739">1739</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Burhan-ul-Mulk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saadat Ali Khan I, titled Burhan-ul-Mulk, was a Shia noble from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> who became the first Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>. Appointed governor of Awadh by the Mughal emperor Muhammad Shah in 1722, he made the province largely autonomous and hereditary. He was captured during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s invasion in 1739 and died soon after; his nephew and son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safdar-jang">Safdar Jang</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saadat-ali-khan-i.jpg" alt="Saadat Ali Khan I" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burhan_ul_Mulk_Sa%27adat_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Mir Muhammad Amin, he was the younger son of Mir Muhammad Nasir, a merchant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, and traced his descent from Musa al-Kadhim, the seventh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> of Shia Islam. His birth date was not recorded; the historian Ashirbadi Lal Srivastava places it around 1680. As the Safavid state declined, the family fell into poverty, and his father and elder brother, Mir Muhammad Baqar, left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in late 1707 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>. He followed in 1708 or 1709, after his father had died.</p><p>In 1709 the brothers went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in search of work. From July 1710 he served Sarbuland Khan, the faujdar of Kara-Manikpur, as camp superintendent. Under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhsiyar">Farrukhsiyar</a> he won a mansab of 1,000 horses, and in 1719 Syed Hussain Ali Khan, one of the Sayyid brothers, made him faujdar of Hindaun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayana">Bayana</a>, where he put down a rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> and Jat zamindars within six months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He then joined the conspiracy against Hussain Ali Khan, who was killed on 8 October 1720; Emperor Muhammad Shah rewarded him with the title Saadat Khan Bahadur, and on 15 October 1720 made him governor of Akbarabad (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>) with the title Burhan-ul-Mulk. There he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and Bharatpur, until Khan Dauran had him dismissed.</p><p>Appointed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> on 9 September 1722, he allied with the sheikhs of Kakori, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gomti-river">Gomti River</a> by night and drove the Shaikhzadas from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>. He reformed the province's revenue, giving the jagirdars a discount on their assessments while making the amils answerable to the governor, which ended the jagirdars' administrative authority over their lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in early 1739, he marched to Karnal with 30,000 horsemen, but in the battle of 23 February his elephant was driven into the Persian ranks and he was captured. He advised Nader Shah to deal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Nizam-ul-Mulk</a>. Later, angered that the Nizam's son was made mir bakshi, he urged him to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. He entered the city on 9 March and died before dawn after the night of 19 to 20 March. Historians disagree on the cause: illness, a cancer of the legs, or poison taken after humiliation by Nader Shah. Through his eldest daughter, married to his nephew Safdar Jung, every later ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> down to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wajid-ali-shah">Wajid Ali Shah</a> descended from him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saadat Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 9 September 1722 <b>Saadat Ali Khan I</b> was appointed Subahdar of Oudh Subah, succeeding Girdhar Bahadur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.21,58.8&amp;year=1718" aria-label="Nishapur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Saadat Ali Khan I"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4917 4469h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4917 4469h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.21,58.8&amp;year=1718">What was here in 1718? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
